\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The scalar result and the gauge transfer question}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The sufficient criterion here comes from the compact-spin
argument of Friedli–Velenik
\citet{Lemma 10.8, pp. 442–443}{friedli2017statistical},
with an explicit Gaussian cutoff
passage. It concerns an ordinary scalar field with a chosen
reflection. The classical small-spacing expansion of the
[Wilson action](fdip-007F) supplies no reflection-positive
probability law by itself. Nor does integrating matter into a
[connection marginal](fdip-0077) establish such a property.}

\p{For gauge fields, the
[positive-character kernel conditions](fdip-007I) give a different
starting point for an explicit finite-volume transfer construction.
One must specify the convolution kernel, gauge projection and
time-boundary convention and prove the operator properties.
The [failure of a quotient shift](fdip-007Q) explains why those
steps cannot be replaced by reflection positivity alone.}

\p{Osterwalder–Seiler \citek{osterwalder1978gauge} provides
historical context for gauge and fermion positivity.
It is cited here for that context only;
no theorem or proof from it is used to extend the scalar result.
General gauge–fermion reflection positivity, an infinite-time
vacuum construction and continuum reconstruction require separate
hypotheses and arguments. Time here remains a chosen lattice
direction, rather than an additional field observable.}
